#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""run-and-wake: run a command detached, then wake the T3 thread that started it.

Usage: run-and-wake [--] CMD [ARGS...]
       run-and-wake -- heavy-gate --wait 7200 -- CMD...
       run-and-wake --recover      (send the wakes a host restart left undelivered; run by run-and-wake-recover.service)
       run-and-wake --wait JOB     (block until JOB ends, print its result line, exit with its status; when run by the session that started JOB, the wake for JOB is then dropped)

Contract (docs/run-and-wake.md):
- Returns at once (unless nothing could wake the caller: see the no-wake-target bullet below). Prints the job id, the job's pid and its output file.
- CMD runs in its own session, so it outlives the tool call or shell that started it.
  Its stdout and stderr go to the output file.
- On Linux with a working `systemd-run --user`, the launcher first re-runs itself in its own transient scope
  (run-and-wake-<job>.scope), so the watcher and the job also outlive the systemd scope of the session that started them
  (T3 stops a Claude session's scope when it releases the session: 30 minutes without a turn, a restart, a force-stop; a setsid session stays in that scope's cgroup).
  Without systemd-run (macOS, the work Mac) or without a user manager, nothing changes: fork and setsid only.
  The scope joins T3's agents slice (app-t3code-agents.slice) when that slice has a memory limit, so a job shares the memory budget
  and CPU weight of the agents instead of escaping them; otherwise it gets T3's own limit formula on the scope itself.
- The watcher forwards SIGTERM, SIGHUP and SIGINT to the job and keeps watching, so a stop that signals the whole group
  still ends in an `end` record (with the job's signal) and a wake.
- When CMD exits (any status), at most one wake is attempted: one `t3 thread send <thread> -` with the result line on stdin, to the T3 Code thread
  in $T3CODE_THREAD_ID captured at launch (CLI: $T3CODE_CLI_PATH, else ~/.t3/bin/t3). A busy thread takes it as its next turn.
  While the T3 server cannot be reached (it is restarting), the send is retried every 5 s for up to 10 minutes: one wake is delivered, not one per try.
  A job that was stopped (a signal reached the watcher) gets one attempt and no wait, so a shutdown is not held up; --recover delivers it.
- With no T3CODE_THREAD_ID (a plain terminal, Codex, or a Pi child that T3 runs as a delegated task) nothing could wake the caller, so the launcher does
  not return at once: it waits for the job as `--wait` does, prints the line the wake would have carried, and exits with the job's status, so the result
  reaches the caller in the same call. The watcher logs a `skip` with reason no-thread.
- A wake that would say nothing new is not sent, and each drop is a `skip` record with its reason:
    waited     a `run-and-wake --wait JOB` run by the thread the wake is for is blocked on the job or already printed its result, so a second message would be a duplicate. A wait from any other thread just prints; the owner's wake is still sent
    cancelled  the session stopped the job itself with `kgate cancel` (kgate's end line has "cancelled": true); `kgate cancel` already printed the end line to its caller
    seen       the job's output file was read after the job ended, so the owner already has the result (`held_s` says how long the wake was held for a busy thread)
    stopped-in-turn  the job was stopped by a signal while its thread was in a turn and nothing read its output: whoever was running when it stopped knows it ended (the record names the signals as `stopped`)
  Every other end, success or failure, still wakes.
- A wake is sent when the thread can act on it, not when the job ends. T3 runs a message sent to a thread that is in the middle of a turn as a new turn after
  that turn, and by then the agent has often handled the job: the reply to that stale wake is a new result for a delegated task and replaces its final report.
  So the watcher asks `t3 thread show` whether the thread has an active run, and while it does, holds the wake and asks again every RUN_AND_WAKE_BUSY_POLL
  seconds (default 30). When the thread is idle (or `thread show` cannot say: it fails, prints nothing or prints something unreadable, and the wake goes out as
  it always did), the wake is dropped as `seen` if the job's output file was read after the job ended (its access time is later than the end), and sent if not.
  The same read check runs just before a wake in a burst is sent. A stopped job is held and checked the same way, and when it was held (its thread was in a turn
  when it stopped) and nothing read its output, it is dropped as `stopped-in-turn`. A stopped job on an idle thread, or on a thread whose state cannot be read,
  sends at once. The held seconds are on the `wake` or `skip` record as `held_s`. A file system that does not update access times, or relatime missing a read
  (it updates the access time only when it is not newer than the last write), means no `seen` and the wake is sent, as before. Accepted risk: a job stopped by
  someone other than its owner while the owner's thread is in a turn gives the owner no wake; each case is a `stopped-in-turn` skip, next to the job's `end` record.
- The sweep (a watcher that died after its job ended) applies the same drop rules before it sends the did-not-finish text: `waited`, `seen` (the output file's
  access time is later than the `end` record's `ts`), and `stopped-in-turn` (the `end` record has `stopped` and one `t3 thread show` says the thread is in a turn).
  The sweep runs inside every run-and-wake call, so it looks once and never waits for a turn. `--recover` (a job from an earlier boot) is unchanged.
- A thread's wakes for jobs that end within RUN_AND_WAKE_COALESCE seconds (default 10; 0 turns it off) of each other are sent as one message, one line per job,
  each line the text the job would have got alone. Each merged job has its own `wake` record (`merged_with` names the others). A job that was stopped
  (a signal reached the watcher) is sent at once.
- 1Password token: when this process has no OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN, the job gets the one in ~/.config/agent-harness/op-service-token
  in its environment, and only there: not in argv, the log, the job record or the job's output. So a job reads 1Password with the
  service account and never the desktop app (playbook 78819cbd). No token file and no variable: the job runs without one.
- A host restart kills the job (nothing survives it) and the T3 server stops in the same shutdown, so the wake cannot be sent then.
  `run-and-wake --recover` (run by run-and-wake-recover.service at boot) sends it once T3 is back, for every T3-thread job that started
  in an earlier boot and has no delivered wake (whatever its `end` says: a shutdown makes wrapper shells exit 0 as the job inside dies): "job did NOT finish: the host restarted at <boot time> and the job was killed. Rerun it".
  Jobs logged before the `boot` field existed are never replayed, and neither are jobs from older logs that carry a herdr `pane` and no thread.
  A watcher killed between the send and the wake record can make recovery send a second copy.
- A job whose watcher is gone (the container or host restarted, or the watcher was killed) is not left with a `start` and nothing else. The watcher holds
  an exclusive lock on jobs/<job>.lock from before `start` until it has woken, then removes the file; a lock nobody holds on a file that is still there means
  the watcher died. Every run-and-wake call (launch, --wait, --recover) sweeps these: it writes `end` with error "lost" (once the job's own process is gone too) and
  wakes the owner with "job did NOT finish ... its status is unknown. Rerun it". --recover waits for T3 as the watcher does; the other calls try once and the next call
  tries again. Jobs started before the lock existed are never swept.
- Every job writes to ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/run-and-wake/log.jsonl, one JSON
  record per line, all carrying the job id and a UTC time:
    start  {cmd, cwd, thread, pid, pid_start, watcher, output, op_token, scope, slice, boot}  (watcher: the watcher's pid; pid_start: when the job's process
           started, so a recycled pid is not taken for it; boot: the kernel boot id, null off Linux; op_token: env, file, or null: where the job's
           1Password token came from; the value is never logged. scope: the systemd scope the job runs in, or null;
           slice: the slice that scope is in, read back from /proc, or null)
    end    {exit, signal, duration_s, stopped?}  (stopped: signals this process forwarded to the job; recover also writes one for a job a restart left
           with no end: exit null, signal null, error "host-restarted", recovered true; a sweep writes one for a job whose watcher is gone: exit null,
           signal null, error "lost", lost true: the job's own status is not known)
    wake   {target: t3, result: sent|failed, error?, duration_s, attempts? (when retried), recover? (sent by --recover), merged_with? (the other jobs in the same message), held_s? (seconds held while the thread was in a turn)}
    read   {owner}                  (a `run-and-wake --wait` printed the job's result; owner: true when it ran in the thread the wake is for)
    skip   {reason, detail?, held_s?, stopped? (the signals, for `stopped-in-turn`)}
  Older logs also hold `pane` and `session` on `start` (a herdr pane that was woken then), and `blocked` records and herdr wakes; they are still read, never written.
"""
import fcntl
import hashlib
import json
import os
import secrets
import shlex
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import traceback
from datetime import datetime, timezone

NAME = "run-and-wake"
STATE = os.path.join(os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME") or os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state"), NAME)
LOG = os.path.join(STATE, "log.jsonl")
TOKEN_FILE = os.path.expanduser("~/.config/agent-harness/op-service-token")
SCOPED = "--scoped"  # internal first argument: the launcher re-ran itself inside its own systemd scope; the job id follows
# --expand-environment=no: systemd-run expands $VAR in the command's arguments, and since 258 not in --scope mode, so ask for the same on every version (254+; older ones fail the probe and keep the plain path).
# OOMPolicy=continue: an OOM kill takes the job, not the watcher that must record it.
SCOPE_ARGS = ["--user", "--scope", "--quiet", "--collect", "--expand-environment=no", "--property=OOMPolicy=continue"]
AGENT_SLICE = "app-t3code-agents.slice"  # T3's agents slice: T3 sets its MemoryHigh and MemoryMax at server start, its parent carries the low CPU weight
GiB = 1024**3
FORWARDED = (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP, signal.SIGINT)
RECOVER = "--recover"
WAIT = "--wait"
SWEEP_LOCK = os.path.join(STATE, "sweep.lock")
SPOOL = os.path.join(STATE, "spool")  # one file per T3 thread: the finished jobs whose wake is waiting for the merge window to close
# The T3 CLI says one of these (exit 1) when no server answers: T3 is restarting or the host is. Anything else it says (thread not found) is an answer.
UNREACHABLE = ("Could not reach the T3 Code server", "No running T3 Code server")
# T3 restarts in seconds; the wake waits this long for it. RUN_AND_WAKE_T3_RETRY=<interval>:<window> (seconds) tunes it.
def t3_retry():
    """(interval, window) in seconds from RUN_AND_WAKE_T3_RETRY=<interval>:<window>; a value that is not two such numbers falls back to 5:600 with a warning."""
    setting = os.environ.get("RUN_AND_WAKE_T3_RETRY") or "5:600"
    try:
        interval, window = (float(x) for x in setting.split(":"))
        if interval > 0 and window >= 0:
            return interval, window
    except ValueError:
        pass
    print(f"[{NAME}] WARN: RUN_AND_WAKE_T3_RETRY={setting!r} is not <interval>:<window> in seconds; using 5:600", file=sys.stderr)
    return 5.0, 600.0


T3_RETRY = t3_retry()


def coalesce_window():
    """Seconds a thread's wakes wait for others that end close behind them: RUN_AND_WAKE_COALESCE=<seconds>, default 10, 0 = off. A bad value means the default, with a warning."""
    setting = os.environ.get("RUN_AND_WAKE_COALESCE")
    if setting is None or setting == "":
        return 10.0
    try:
        window = float(setting)
        if window >= 0:
            return window
    except ValueError:
        pass
    print(f"[{NAME}] WARN: RUN_AND_WAKE_COALESCE={setting!r} is not a number of seconds; using 10", file=sys.stderr)
    return 10.0


def busy_poll():
    """Seconds between asks whether a busy thread is idle yet: RUN_AND_WAKE_BUSY_POLL=<seconds>, default 30. A bad value means the default, with a warning."""
    setting = os.environ.get("RUN_AND_WAKE_BUSY_POLL")
    if setting is None or setting == "":
        return 30.0
    try:
        poll = float(setting)
        if poll > 0:
            return poll
    except ValueError:
        pass
    print(f"[{NAME}] WARN: RUN_AND_WAKE_BUSY_POLL={setting!r} is not a positive number of seconds; using 30", file=sys.stderr)
    return 30.0


def say(level, message):
    print(f"[{NAME}] {level}: {message}", flush=True)


def utc():
    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="milliseconds").replace("+00:00", "Z")


def record(job, event, **fields):
    line = json.dumps({"ts": utc(), "job": job, "event": event, **fields}, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n"
    with open(LOG, "a", encoding="utf-8") as log:
        log.write(line)


def human(seconds):
    s = int(round(seconds))
    if s < 60:
        return f"{s}s"
    if s < 3600:
        return f"{s // 60}m{s % 60:02d}s"
    return f"{s // 3600}h{s % 3600 // 60:02d}m"


def boot_id():
    """The kernel's id for this boot (Linux), else None. A job that carries another one was cut off by a host restart."""
    try:
        with open("/proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id") as f:
            return f.read().strip() or None
    except OSError:
        return None


def boot_time():
    """When this boot began, UTC, from /proc/stat."""
    with open("/proc/stat") as f:
        for line in f:
            if line.startswith("btime "):
                return datetime.fromtimestamp(int(line.split()[1]), timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")
    return "an unknown time"


def message(job, status, seconds, cmd, output):
    what = " ".join(shlex.join(cmd).split())
    return f"[{NAME} k:{job}] job finished: {status} after {human(seconds)}: {what}. Output: {output}"


# --- T3 (the one external system) ---

def t3_cli():
    return os.environ.get("T3CODE_CLI_PATH") or os.path.expanduser("~/.t3/bin/t3")


def t3_send(thread, text):
    """One `t3 thread send`: the message goes on stdin, as the coordinator's deliverer sends it. None when sent, else the error."""
    try:
        done = subprocess.run([t3_cli(), "thread", "send", thread, "-"], input=text, capture_output=True, text=True)
    except OSError as err:
        return f"t3-missing: {err}"
    return None if done.returncode == 0 else f"exit {done.returncode}: {(done.stderr or done.stdout).strip()}"


def t3_unreachable(error):
    return error is not None and any(marker in error for marker in UNREACHABLE)


def thread_in_a_turn(thread):
    """True when `t3 thread show` says the thread has an active run. Anything else (the CLI missing, T3 down, no answer in a minute, an answer that is not
    the thread's JSON) is False: a wake that cannot be placed is sent, as it always was."""
    try:
        done = subprocess.run([t3_cli(), "thread", "show", "--json", "--messages", "0", thread], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        if done.returncode != 0:
            return False
        return bool(json.loads(done.stdout)["thread"]["activeRunId"])
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
        return False


def t3_send_when_up(thread, text, deadline):
    """Send; while no T3 server answers, try again every interval until the deadline. (error or None, attempts)."""
    interval = T3_RETRY[0]
    attempts = 0
    while True:
        attempts += 1
        error = t3_send(thread, text)
        if not t3_unreachable(error) or time.monotonic() + interval > deadline:
            return error, attempts
        time.sleep(interval)  # a stop signal now ends the watcher (its handlers are back to default): a shutdown is not held up


def record_wake(job, started, error, attempts, **extra):
    took = round(time.monotonic() - started, 3)
    more = {"attempts": attempts} if attempts > 1 else {}
    if error is None:
        record(job, "wake", target="t3", result="sent", duration_s=took, **more, **extra)
    else:
        record(job, "wake", target="t3", result="failed", error=error, duration_s=took, **more, **extra)


def wake_thread(job, thread, text, stopped, **extra):
    """One wake. A job that was stopped (a shutdown, a scope stop) gets one attempt and no wait: the stop may be the host going down, and a
    watcher still waiting would hold the shutdown for the scope's stop timeout. --recover owes the wake then."""
    started = time.monotonic()
    error, attempts = t3_send_when_up(thread, text, started if stopped else started + T3_RETRY[1])
    record_wake(job, started, error, attempts, **extra)


# --- wakes that would say nothing new: dropped, or merged ---

def kgate_log():
    return os.path.join(os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME") or os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state"), "kgate", "log.jsonl")  # the path kgate writes


def kgate_cancelled(job):
    """True when kgate's log ends this job's run with "cancelled": true: the session stopped it itself with `kgate cancel` (a caller cancels its own job id only),
    and `kgate cancel` printed that end line to it. kgate writes the line before its client exits, so it is there when the watcher looks. Reads the last megabyte."""
    try:
        with open(kgate_log(), "rb") as f:
            f.seek(0, os.SEEK_END)
            f.seek(max(0, f.tell() - 1_000_000))
            tail = f.read().decode(errors="replace")
    except OSError:
        return False
    for line in reversed(tail.splitlines()):
        if job in line and '"cancelled"' in line:
            try:
                r = json.loads(line)
            except ValueError:
                continue
            if isinstance(r, dict) and r.get("job") == job and r.get("cancelled") is True:
                return True
    return False


def caller():
    """The T3 thread this process runs in, or None."""
    return os.environ.get("T3CODE_THREAD_ID") or None


def wait_file(job):
    return os.path.join(STATE, "jobs", job + ".wait")


def read_marker(job):
    return os.path.join(STATE, "jobs", job + ".read")


def waiter_alive(job):
    """True while a `--wait` process holds the job's wait lock (a shared lock: the kernel drops it when the waiter dies, so a dead waiter never hides a wake)."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(wait_file(job), os.O_RDONLY)
    except OSError:
        return False
    try:
        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        return False
    except OSError:
        return True
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def result_read(job):
    """The waiter is blocked on the job, or has printed its result: the session already has it."""
    return os.path.exists(read_marker(job)) or waiter_alive(job)


def output_path(job):
    return os.path.join(STATE, "jobs", job + ".log")


def output_read(job, ended):
    """The job's output file was read after the job ended (epoch seconds): its access time is later. A file system that keeps no access times, or relatime
    skipping this read because an earlier one is newer than the last write, says False, and the wake goes out."""
    try:
        return ended is not None and os.stat(output_path(job)).st_atime > ended
    except OSError:
        return False


def settled(job, ended):
    """(reason, detail) when the owner already has this job's result and the wake would say nothing new, else None."""
    if result_read(job):
        return "waited", "a run-and-wake --wait delivered the result"
    if output_read(job, ended):
        return "seen", "the job's output was read after the job ended"
    return None


def stopped_in_turn(stopped, in_a_turn):
    """(reason, detail) for a job that a signal stopped while its thread was in a turn: whoever was running then knows it ended, and a wake would run as a stale turn."""
    if stopped and in_a_turn:
        return "stopped-in-turn", f"stopped by {', '.join(stopped)} while the thread was in a turn"
    return None


def skip_fields(held, stopped):
    return {**held_field(held), **({"stopped": stopped} if stopped else {})}


def hold_while_in_a_turn(thread):
    """Seconds the wake waited for the thread to leave its turn, 0.0 when it was not in one. A message sent to a thread in a turn runs as a new turn
    after it, when it is stale."""
    if not thread_in_a_turn(thread):
        return 0.0
    started = time.monotonic()
    poll = busy_poll()
    while True:
        time.sleep(poll)
        if not thread_in_a_turn(thread):
            return time.monotonic() - started


def held_field(held):
    return {"held_s": round(held, 1)} if held > 0 else {}


class FlushNow(Exception):
    """A stop signal reached a watcher that was waiting for the merge window: send now, the host may be going down."""


def raise_flush(_signum, _frame):
    raise FlushNow()


def wake_thread_merged(job, thread, text, window, ended, held):
    """Queue this job's wake for its thread and, after `window` seconds, send everything queued as one message. Whichever watcher wakes first sends the whole
    queue and writes every merged job's `wake` record; a watcher that finds its job already sent has nothing left to do. The queue lock is held across the send, so a
    job that ends while T3 is being waited for joins the next message, not a second concurrent send."""
    key = hashlib.sha1(thread.encode()).hexdigest()[:16]
    os.makedirs(SPOOL, exist_ok=True)
    spool, lock = os.path.join(SPOOL, key + ".jsonl"), os.path.join(SPOOL, key + ".lock")
    with open(lock, "a") as guard:
        fcntl.flock(guard, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        with open(spool, "a", encoding="utf-8") as queue:
            queue.write(json.dumps({"job": job, "text": text, "ended": ended, "held": held}, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")
    flush_now = False
    handled = [signum for signum in FORWARDED if signal.getsignal(signum) is not signal.SIG_IGN]
    for signum in handled:
        signal.signal(signum, raise_flush)
    try:
        time.sleep(window)
    except FlushNow:
        flush_now = True
    finally:
        for signum in handled:
            signal.signal(signum, signal.SIG_DFL)
    with open(lock, "a") as guard:
        fcntl.flock(guard, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        try:
            with open(spool, encoding="utf-8") as queue:
                queued = [json.loads(line) for line in queue if line.strip()]
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            queued = []
        if not any(e.get("job") == job for e in queued):
            return  # a watcher that woke before us has sent it
        send = []
        for e in queued:
            done = settled(e["job"], e.get("ended"))
            if done:
                record(e["job"], "skip", reason=done[0], detail=done[1], **held_field(e.get("held") or 0))
            else:
                send.append(e)
        if send:
            started = time.monotonic()
            error, attempts = t3_send_when_up(thread, "\n".join(e["text"] for e in send), started if flush_now else started + T3_RETRY[1])
            for e in send:
                record_wake(e["job"], started, error, attempts, **held_field(e.get("held") or 0), **({"merged_with": [o["job"] for o in send if o is not e]} if len(send) > 1 else {}))
        open(spool, "w").close()


def deliver(job, thread, text, stopped, ended):
    """Send this job's wake once the thread can act on it, unless it would say nothing new (see the docstring)."""
    if kgate_cancelled(job):
        record(job, "skip", reason="cancelled", detail="stopped by kgate cancel, which printed its end line to the caller")
    elif result_read(job):
        record(job, "skip", reason="waited", detail="a run-and-wake --wait delivered the result")
    elif not thread:
        record(job, "skip", reason="no-thread", detail="no T3CODE_THREAD_ID: nothing to wake")
    else:
        held = hold_while_in_a_turn(thread)
        done = settled(job, ended) or stopped_in_turn(stopped, held > 0)
        if done:
            record(job, "skip", reason=done[0], detail=done[1], **skip_fields(held, stopped))
            return
        window = coalesce_window()
        if stopped or window <= 0:
            wake_thread(job, thread, text, stopped, **held_field(held))
        else:
            wake_thread_merged(job, thread, text, window, ended, held)


# --- launch ---

def tell(report, text):
    """Best effort: the launcher may already be gone (its tool call ended), and the job must still be watched."""
    try:
        os.write(report, text.encode())
    except OSError:
        pass
    finally:
        os.close(report)


def job_env(job):
    """(environment, source): ours plus the 1Password token from its file when we have none. source: env, file or None."""
    env = {**os.environ, "RUN_AND_WAKE_JOB": job}
    if env.get("OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN"):
        return env, "env"
    try:
        with open(TOKEN_FILE) as f:
            token = f.read().strip()
    except OSError:
        token = ""
    if not token:
        return env, None
    env["OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN"] = token
    return env, "file"


def watch(job, cmd, output, thread, report, scope, slice_):
    """Runs in the detached process: start the job, wait for it, then wake."""
    started = time.monotonic()
    guard = open(lock_path(job), "a")  # held until this process exits (an open file is not inherited by the job): a lock nobody holds means the watcher died
    fcntl.flock(guard, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
    env, op_token = job_env(job)
    jobs = []
    stopped = []  # the signals that reached us while the job ran: a job that was stopped did not run to its own end

    def forward(signum, _frame):
        stopped.append(signal.Signals(signum).name)
        # A stop that signals the whole group reaches the watcher too: keep watching so the job's end is recorded and woken.
        # (A Python handler, not SIG_IGN: an ignored signal would be inherited by the job.)
        for running in jobs:
            try:
                running.send_signal(signum)
            except OSError:
                pass

    # A signal this process was started ignoring (nohup's SIGHUP) stays ignored, for the watcher and for the job that inherits it, as before.
    handled = [signum for signum in FORWARDED if signal.getsignal(signum) is not signal.SIG_IGN]
    for signum in handled:
        signal.signal(signum, forward)
    with open(output, "ab", buffering=0) as out:
        try:
            child = subprocess.Popen(cmd, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=out, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, env=env)
        except OSError as error:
            record(job, "start", cmd=cmd, cwd=os.getcwd(), thread=thread, pid=None, output=output, op_token=op_token, scope=scope, slice=slice_, boot=boot_id())
            tell(report, f"failed {error}")
            out.write(f"[{NAME}] ERROR: could not start: {error}\n".encode())
            record(job, "end", exit=127, signal=None, error=str(error), duration_s=0.0)
            status = f"could not start ({error})"
            ended = time.time()
        else:
            jobs.append(child)
            record(job, "start", cmd=cmd, cwd=os.getcwd(), thread=thread, pid=child.pid, pid_start=proc_start(child.pid), watcher=os.getpid(), output=output, op_token=op_token, scope=scope, slice=slice_, boot=boot_id())
            tell(report, f"pid {child.pid}")
            code = child.wait()
            ended = time.time()
            for signum in handled:  # nothing left to forward to: a signal now ends the wake wait as it always did
                signal.signal(signum, signal.SIG_DFL)
            sig = signal.Signals(-code).name if code < 0 else None
            record(job, "end", exit=code if code >= 0 else None, signal=sig, duration_s=round(time.monotonic() - started, 3), **({"stopped": stopped} if stopped else {}))
            status = f"killed by {sig}" if sig else f"exit {code}"
    deliver(job, thread, message(job, status, time.monotonic() - started, cmd, output), stopped, ended)
    try:
        os.unlink(lock_path(job))  # the job is settled: a sweep has nothing to look at
    except OSError:
        pass


def agent_slice_limited(systemctl):
    """True when T3's agents slice exists with a finite MemoryMax. T3 sets it at server start, so it is absent when T3 has not run since boot."""
    if not systemctl:
        return False
    try:
        done = subprocess.run([systemctl, "--user", "show", AGENT_SLICE, "--property=MemoryMax", "--value"],
                              stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return False
    return done.returncode == 0 and done.stdout.strip().isdigit()  # "infinity" when unset


def own_memory_limits():
    """T3's agent-slice formula (agentSliceMemoryLimits): leave 6 GiB before throttling and 4 GiB before a kill, at least half and three quarters of RAM."""
    total = os.sysconf("SC_PAGE_SIZE") * os.sysconf("SC_PHYS_PAGES")
    return max(total - 6 * GiB, total // 2), max(total - 4 * GiB, total * 3 // 4)


def scope_runner():
    """The systemd-run argv prefix when this host can start a user scope (Linux with a user manager), else None. The probe is
    the launch itself with `true` for the command: an exec cannot fall back, so check first. macOS and hosts without a user manager keep the plain path.
    Placement: in T3's agents slice when that slice carries a memory limit, so a job shares the agents' memory budget and CPU weight
    (a job in app.slice would escape both: a heavy job could take the box down); when it does not (T3 has not started since boot),
    the same limits go on the scope itself."""
    run = shutil.which("systemd-run") if sys.platform.startswith("linux") else None
    if not run:
        return None
    if agent_slice_limited(shutil.which("systemctl")):
        place = [f"--slice={AGENT_SLICE}"]
    else:
        high, top = own_memory_limits()
        place = [f"--property=MemoryHigh={high}", f"--property=MemoryMax={top}"]
    argv = [run, *SCOPE_ARGS, *place]
    try:
        done = subprocess.run([*argv, "--", "true"], stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, capture_output=True, timeout=10)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    return argv if done.returncode == 0 else None


def own_slice():
    """The slice this process's cgroup sits in, read back from /proc (what the kernel put us in, not what we asked for), or None."""
    try:
        with open("/proc/self/cgroup") as cgroup:
            path = cgroup.read().strip().rsplit("::", 1)[1]
    except (OSError, IndexError):
        return None
    parts = path.rstrip("/").split("/")
    return parts[-2] if len(parts) > 1 and parts[-2].endswith(".slice") else None


# --- recovery: wakes a host restart (or a T3 outage longer than the watcher waited) left undelivered ---

def read_jobs():
    """The log as {job: [records]}; a line that is not a JSON record (a write cut by the shutdown, or anything else) is skipped."""
    jobs = {}
    try:
        with open(LOG, encoding="utf-8") as log:
            lines = log.readlines()
    except OSError:
        return jobs
    for line in lines:
        try:
            r = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        if isinstance(r, dict) and isinstance(r.get("job"), str) and isinstance(r.get("event"), str):
            jobs.setdefault(r["job"], []).append(r)
    return jobs


def owed(rs, boot):
    """What this job still owes its thread: "lost" (it is from an earlier boot: send the did-not-finish text, whatever its end record says: a
    shutdown makes wrappers exit 0 as the job inside dies), "late" (this boot: it ended but T3 stayed down past the window: send the normal text) or None. Only T3-thread jobs that carry a boot id: older ones, and pane jobs from older logs, are never replayed."""
    start = next((r for r in rs if r["event"] == "start"), None)
    if not start or not start.get("boot") or not start.get("thread"):
        return None
    wakes = [r for r in rs if r["event"] == "wake"]
    if any(r["event"] == "skip" for r in rs) or any(w["result"] == "sent" for w in wakes):
        return None
    if any(w["result"] == "failed" and not t3_unreachable(w.get("error")) for w in wakes):
        return None  # T3 answered and refused (thread gone): trying again changes nothing
    end = next((r for r in rs if r["event"] == "end"), None)
    if start["boot"] != boot:
        return "lost"
    return "late" if wakes and end else None  # this boot: only a wake that found T3 down; a job still running is not ours


def status_of(end):
    return f"killed by {end['signal']}" if end.get("signal") else f"exit {end.get('exit')}"


def lost_message(job, start, end, restarted):
    what = " ".join(shlex.join(start["cmd"]).split())
    text = (f"[{NAME} k:{job}] job did NOT finish: the host restarted at {restarted} and the job was killed. Rerun it; this is not a result. "
            f"Started {start['ts']}: {what}. Partial output: {start['output']}.")
    if end and not end.get("recovered") and not end.get("lost"):
        text += f" Its last record, an end at {end['ts']} ({status_of(end)}), is not a result either."
    return text


def recover(wait=None, quiet=False, only=None):
    """Send the wake of every job that owes one. Idempotent: a job with a delivered wake is skipped, and a lock keeps two runs apart.
    `wait`: seconds to wait for T3 (default: the retry window). `quiet`: print nothing (a sweep runs it inside another call).
    `only`: a set of job ids, the only jobs to wake (a sweep passes the jobs it found, so jobs logged before the lock existed stay quiet)."""
    say = (lambda *_: None) if quiet else globals()["say"]
    boot = boot_id()
    if not boot:
        say("INFO", "no boot id on this host: nothing to recover")
        return 0
    with open(os.path.join(STATE, "recover.lock"), "w") as lock:
        try:
            fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        except OSError:
            say("INFO", "another --recover is running")
            return 0
        jobs = read_jobs()
        todo = sorted(((rs[0]["ts"], job, rs, kind) for job, rs in jobs.items() if (only is None or job in only) and (kind := owed(rs, boot))), key=lambda t: t[0])
        deadline = time.monotonic() + (T3_RETRY[1] if wait is None else wait)  # one wait for T3 for the whole run, not one per job
        undelivered = 0
        for _, job, rs, kind in todo:
            start = next(r for r in rs if r["event"] == "start")
            end = next((r for r in rs if r["event"] == "end"), None)
            if kind == "lost":
                if not end:
                    record(job, "end", exit=None, signal=None, duration_s=None, error="host-restarted", recovered=True)
                text = lost_message(job, start, end, boot_time())
            else:
                text = message(job, status_of(end), end.get("duration_s") or 0, start["cmd"], start["output"]) + " (delivered late: T3 was not reachable when the job ended.)"
            started = time.monotonic()
            error, attempts = t3_send_when_up(start["thread"], text, deadline)
            record_wake(job, started, error, attempts, recover=True)
            if error is None:
                say("INFO", f"job {job}: {kind}, woke thread {start['thread']}")
            else:
                undelivered += t3_unreachable(error)
                say("ERROR", f"job {job}: {kind}, wake failed: {error}")
        if not todo:
            say("INFO", "no job owes a wake")
        return 1 if undelivered else 0


def lock_path(job):
    return os.path.join(STATE, "jobs", job + ".lock")


def proc_start(pid):
    """An opaque stamp of when process `pid` started (Linux: /proc start ticks, else `ps lstart`), None when there is no such process. A recycled pid has another stamp."""
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/stat") as f:
            fields = f.read().rsplit(")", 1)[1].split()  # state, then the rest: start ticks is field 22 of the file
        return None if fields[0] == "Z" else fields[19]  # a zombie is gone: nothing runs
    except (OSError, IndexError):
        pass
    if os.path.isdir("/proc/self"):
        return None  # Linux, and no such process
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(["ps", "-o", "lstart=", "-p", str(pid)], capture_output=True, text=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=10)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    return done.stdout.strip() or None


def job_running(start):
    """The job's own process is still there. This namespace's pids only: from another container's pid space a live job reads as gone, which is why a sweep acts only once the watcher is gone too."""
    pid = start.get("pid")
    if not isinstance(pid, int) or pid <= 0:
        return False
    began = start.get("pid_start")
    return pid_alive(pid) if began is None else proc_start(pid) == began


def watcher_gone(job):
    """True when the job has a lock file nobody holds: its watcher died (a normal watcher removes the file when it is done)."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(lock_path(job), os.O_RDONLY)
    except OSError:
        return False
    try:
        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        return True
    except OSError:
        return False
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def wake_owed(rs):
    """A job still owes its owner a wake: none was sent, none was dropped (a skip), and none was refused (T3 answered, so trying again changes nothing)."""
    wakes = [r for r in rs if r["event"] == "wake"]
    if any(r["event"] == "skip" for r in rs) or any(w["result"] == "sent" for w in wakes):
        return False
    return not any(w["result"] == "failed" and not t3_unreachable(w.get("error")) for w in wakes)


def lost_text(job, start):
    what = " ".join(shlex.join(start["cmd"]).split())
    return (f"[{NAME} k:{job}] job did NOT finish: its watcher is gone and sent no result (the container or host restarted, or something killed it), "
            f"so its status is unknown. Rerun it; this is not a result. Started {start['ts']}: {what}. Partial output: {start['output']}.")


def epoch(ts):
    """Seconds since the epoch of a log record's `ts`, None when it is not one."""
    try:
        return datetime.fromisoformat(ts.replace("Z", "+00:00")).timestamp()
    except (AttributeError, ValueError):
        return None


def send_owed(job, start, end, text, deadline):
    """The one wake a swept job is owed, or the reason it is not sent. True when settled, False when T3 was unreachable and the wake is still owed.
    The same drop rules as the watcher's, read once: a sweep runs inside every run-and-wake call and never waits for a turn."""
    stopped = (end or {}).get("stopped") or []
    done = settled(job, epoch((end or {}).get("ts")))
    if not done and stopped and start.get("thread"):
        done = stopped_in_turn(stopped, thread_in_a_turn(start["thread"]))
    if done:
        record(job, "skip", reason=done[0], detail=done[1], **skip_fields(0, stopped))
        return True
    if start.get("thread"):
        started = time.monotonic()
        error, attempts = t3_send_when_up(start["thread"], text, deadline)
        record_wake(job, started, error, attempts, swept=True)
        return not t3_unreachable(error)
    record(job, "skip", reason="no-thread", detail="no T3 thread to wake")  # a job from an older log that carries a herdr pane and no thread
    return True


def earlier_boot(start):
    """The job started in a boot before this one (a host restart, or the pod on another node): --recover tells its thread, and the sweep leaves that to it."""
    return bool(start.get("boot") and boot_id() and start["boot"] != boot_id())


def sweep_job(job, deadline):
    """One job with a lock file. Returns "owed" (its wake found T3 down), "recover" (an earlier-boot thread job that --recover owes a wake) or None (settled, or nothing to do)."""
    if not watcher_gone(job):
        return None
    rs = read_jobs().get(job, [])  # read now: the watcher may have finished normally since the lock was listed
    start = next((r for r in rs if r["event"] == "start"), None)
    if not start:
        return None  # the watcher is about to write it
    owed = wake_owed(rs)
    if earlier_boot(start) and start.get("thread"):
        if owed:
            return "recover"
    else:
        end = next((r for r in rs if r["event"] == "end"), None)
        if not end:
            if job_running(start):
                return None
            record(job, "end", exit=None, signal=None, duration_s=None, error="lost", lost=True)
        # Whatever the watcher recorded, its job's status is not passed on: a watcher that died before it woke may have been stopped by a shutdown, whose exit status is not a result.
        if owed and not send_owed(job, start, end, lost_text(job, start), deadline):
            return "owed"
    try:
        os.unlink(lock_path(job))
    except OSError:
        pass
    return None


def sweep(wait=0.0):
    """End and wake every job whose watcher is gone. A watcher holds jobs/<job>.lock until it has woken, then removes it, so a lock file nobody holds is a dead watcher
    (a container restart kills the watcher with the rest: the pod's pid 1 exiting takes every process). The job's own process must be gone too, or it is left running.
    No `end` yet: write one (error "lost", exit and signal null: its status is not known). Then one did-not-finish wake is sent, trying T3 for `wait` seconds (0: once,
    and the next sweep tries again); a recorded status is never sent as a result. A job from an earlier boot is left to --recover, which the sweep runs for the jobs it found and no others (once, quietly).
    A job whose sweep raises gets an `error` record and the others go on. Returns how many wakes are still owed because T3 was unreachable."""
    try:
        names = sorted(n[:-5] for n in os.listdir(os.path.join(STATE, "jobs")) if n.endswith(".lock"))
    except OSError:
        return 0
    if not names:
        return 0
    deadline = time.monotonic() + wait
    left, for_recover = 0, set()
    with open(SWEEP_LOCK, "a") as guard:
        try:
            fcntl.flock(guard, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        except OSError:
            return 0  # another sweep is at it
        for job in names:
            try:
                outcome = sweep_job(job, deadline)
            except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001
                record_error(job, error)
                continue
            left += outcome == "owed"
            if outcome == "recover":
                for_recover.add(job)
    if for_recover:
        left += recover(wait, quiet=True, only=for_recover)
    return left


def record_error(job, error):
    """A sweep that failed leaves a record, so a sweep that fails on every call shows in the log."""
    try:
        record(job, "error", step="sweep", error=repr(error))
    except OSError:
        say("WARN", f"sweep failed and the log could not be written: {error!r}")


def sweep_quietly(wait=0.0):
    """A sweep that never stops the call it rides on."""
    try:
        return sweep(wait)
    except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001
        record_error("run-and-wake", error)
        return 0


def wait_command(job):
    """The no-poll wait the builder Stop hook names for a run-and-wake job (run_and_wake_wait_hint in dot_local/share/agent-harness/hooks/stop_running_jobs.py).
    tests/run-and-wake checks that the two agree."""
    return "%s %s %s" % (NAME, WAIT, job)


def end_of(job, offset):
    """(the job's `end` record or None, the log offset to resume from). Reads only the lines added since `offset`, whole lines only."""
    try:
        with open(LOG, "rb") as log:
            log.seek(offset)
            chunk = log.read()
    except OSError:
        return None, offset
    complete = chunk[: chunk.rfind(b"\n") + 1]
    marker = ('"job": "%s"' % job).encode()
    found = None
    for line in complete.splitlines():
        if marker in line and b'"end"' in line:
            try:
                r = json.loads(line)
            except ValueError:
                continue
            if isinstance(r, dict) and r.get("job") == job and r.get("event") == "end":
                found = r
    return found, offset + len(complete)


def pid_alive(pid):
    if not isinstance(pid, int) or pid <= 0:
        return False
    try:
        os.kill(pid, 0)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        return False
    except OSError:
        return True
    return True


def wait_for(job):
    """`run-and-wake --wait JOB`: block until the job ends, print the line its wake would carry, exit with the job's status (128+n for a signal). While it blocks it holds
    the job's wait lock, and it leaves a marker when it prints, so the watcher drops the wake that would repeat this line. A job that cannot end (its host restarted, its watcher died) says so and exits 1."""
    start = next((r for r in read_jobs().get(job, []) if r["event"] == "start"), None)
    if not start:
        say("ERROR", f"no job {job} in {LOG}")
        return 64
    os.makedirs(os.path.join(STATE, "jobs"), exist_ok=True)
    me = caller()
    mine = me is not None and me == start.get("thread")  # only the thread the wake is for can stand in for it
    guard = None
    if mine:
        guard = open(wait_file(job), "a")
        fcntl.flock(guard, fcntl.LOCK_SH)  # held until this process exits
    offset, gone = 0, 0
    while True:
        sweep_quietly()
        end, offset = end_of(job, offset)
        if end and end.get("lost"):
            print(lost_text(job, start), flush=True)
            return 1
        if end and not end.get("recovered"):
            break
        if (start.get("boot") and boot_id() and start["boot"] != boot_id()) or (end and end.get("recovered")):
            print(f"[{NAME} k:{job}] job did NOT finish: the host restarted and the job was killed. Rerun it; this is not a result. Started {start['ts']}: {' '.join(shlex.join(start['cmd']).split())}. Partial output: {start['output']}.", flush=True)
            return 1
        gone = 0 if pid_alive(start.get("pid")) else gone + 1
        if gone >= 4:  # the watcher records `end` within milliseconds of the job's exit, so four silent seconds mean it died
            print(f"[{NAME} k:{job}] job has no end record and its process is gone: the watcher died. Treat it as not finished. Output: {start['output']}", flush=True)
            return 1
        time.sleep(1)
    if mine:
        with open(read_marker(job), "w"):
            pass
    record(job, "read", owner=mine)
    status = f"could not start ({end['error']})" if end.get("error") else status_of(end)
    print(message(job, status, end.get("duration_s") or 0, start["cmd"], start["output"]), flush=True)
    if end.get("exit") is not None:
        return end["exit"] & 0xFF
    return 128 + signal.Signals[end["signal"]].value if end.get("signal") in signal.Signals.__members__ else 1


def main(argv):
    if argv[:1] == ["--"]:
        argv = argv[1:]
    if argv[:1] in (["-h"], ["--help"]):
        print(__doc__.strip())
        return 0
    if argv[:1] == [RECOVER]:
        os.makedirs(STATE, exist_ok=True)
        recovered = recover()
        owed = sweep_quietly(T3_RETRY[1])  # even when recover could not reach T3
        return recovered or (1 if owed else 0)
    if argv[:1] == [WAIT]:
        if len(argv) != 2 or argv[1].startswith("-"):
            print(__doc__.strip(), file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        return wait_for(argv[1])
    scoped = argv[:1] == [SCOPED] and len(argv) > 2
    if scoped:
        job, argv = argv[1], argv[2:]
    if not argv or argv[0].startswith("-"):
        print(__doc__.strip(), file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    os.makedirs(os.path.join(STATE, "jobs"), exist_ok=True)
    if not scoped:
        sweep_quietly()  # the jobs a restart or a killed watcher left without an end get one, and their owners their wake
        job = time.strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ", time.gmtime()) + "-" + secrets.token_hex(3)
        run = scope_runner()
        if run:  # become the launcher inside our own scope: same env, cwd and stdio, a cgroup the caller's scope does not own
            try:
                os.execv(run[0], [*run, f"--unit={NAME}-{job}", f"--description={NAME} job {job}", "--",
                               sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__), SCOPED, job, *argv])
            except OSError:
                pass  # no scope after all: run in the caller's cgroup, as before
    scope = f"{NAME}-{job}.scope" if scoped else None
    slice_ = own_slice() if scoped else None
    output = os.path.join(STATE, "jobs", job + ".log")
    thread = caller()
    read, report = os.pipe()
    if os.fork() == 0:  # the watcher: its own session, so killing the launcher's process group leaves it alone
        os.close(read)
        os.setsid()
        null = os.open(os.devnull, os.O_RDWR)
        logfd = os.open(output, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_APPEND, 0o644)
        os.dup2(null, 0)
        os.dup2(logfd, 1)
        os.dup2(logfd, 2)
        try:
            watch(job, argv, output, thread, report, scope, slice_)
        except BaseException:
            traceback.print_exc()  # into the job's output file
        finally:
            os._exit(0)
    os.close(report)
    with os.fdopen(read) as pipe:
        started = pipe.read()
    if started.startswith("pid "):
        say("INFO", f"job {job} started, {started}, output {output}")
    else:
        say("ERROR", f"job {job} could not start: {started[7:] or 'unknown'}; output {output}")
    if thread and started.startswith("pid "):
        say("INFO", f"when it ends, T3 thread {thread} gets one message tagged [{NAME} k:{job}]. Builders: wait with `{wait_command(job)}` in Bash (run_in_background), then t3_background_wait; it prints the result and the message is then not sent. The Stop hook refuses to stop while the job runs. Other seats can end the turn.")
    elif thread:
        say("INFO", f"when it ends, T3 thread {thread} gets one message tagged [{NAME} k:{job}]; you can end your turn")
    else:
        # Nothing can wake this caller (no T3 thread): a Pi child that T3 runs is exactly this. Detaching would end its turn
        # with a result nobody delivers, so wait here instead, as --wait does.
        if started.startswith("pid "):
            say("WARN", f"no T3CODE_THREAD_ID: nothing could wake you when it ends, so this waits for the job and prints its result. "
                        f"The job runs in its own session: if this call is cut off, it keeps running; get its result with `{wait_command(job)}`")
            if scope:
                say("INFO", f"scope {scope} in {slice_ or 'no slice'}: the job outlives the scope of the session that started it")
            sys.stdout.flush()
            return wait_for(job)
    if scope:
        say("INFO", f"scope {scope} in {slice_ or 'no slice'}: the job outlives the scope of the session that started it")
    say("INFO", f"log {LOG}")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
